\section*{About the Paper}
In the original paper 'The macroeconomic effects of oil supply news: Evidence from OPEC announcements’ D. Känzig investigates the effects of the oil supply news shocks on the oil prices and key macroeconomic indicators for the world and US. To identify a structural oil supply news shock, the author captures the variation in high-frequency oil futures prices around the time of regular announcements from OPEC. 

The data is constrained to the period 1983-2017. The author uses OPEC press releases for the period, which amounts to 119 announcements. 'Based on this data, D. Känzig constructs a series of oil supply surprises by taking the (log) difference of the futures price on the day of the OPEC announcement and the price on the last trading day before the announcement.' The daily surprises are aggregated to a monthly series. 'In particular, I use the first principal component of the surprises based on WTI crude futures contracts with maturities ranging from one month to one year.'

For the estimation, 


Methodology: the author uses an oil market VAR model

The findings show that negative news shocks lead to an increase in oil prices, a fall in world oil production and an increase in world oil inventories. Relevant to the U.S. economy, their industrial production falls and consumer prices rise significantly. The paper also finds that oil supply news contribute to historical variations in the oil price.


